# Chapter 13 checkpoint. Start InventoryService, then the app:
#   dotnet run --project InventoryService
#   dotnet run --project AdventureWorks
# (two terminals, both from this folder; in Visual Studio, set
# both as startup projects). Each "Expect" line is the status
# you should see. Send them in order. The order the file places
# is cancelled and then deleted, so it can run again.

@host = https://localhost:7001

###
# The orders collection belongs to a signed-in customer.
# Expect 401
GET {{host}}/api/orders

###
# casey. Expect 200
POST {{host}}/dev/sign-in/casey

###
# Expect 200
# @name token
GET {{host}}/api/csrf

###
# Page 1 of casey's eleven orders, five per page. The Link
# header points to the next page.
# Expect 200 with "totalPages": 3, and it has a Link header
GET {{host}}/api/orders?page=1&pageSize=5

###
# The last page links back only.
# Expect 200 with "page": 3, and it has a Link header
GET {{host}}/api/orders?page=3&pageSize=5

###
# Pages hold 1 to 50 orders. Expect 400
GET {{host}}/api/orders?pageSize=500

###
# One order: an OrderDto with no owner ID, and its version as
# the ETag. Expect 200 with "orderNumber": "AW-0001", and it has
# an ETag header, and the body lacks "ownerId"
# @name order
GET {{host}}/api/orders/1

###
# A change without If-Match. Expect 428
PATCH {{host}}/api/orders/1
Content-Type: application/json
X-CSRF-TOKEN: {{token.response.body.$.token}}

{ "notes": "Leave it at the side door." }

###
# With the ETag just read.
# Expect 200 with "notes": "Leave it at the side door."
# @name patched
PATCH {{host}}/api/orders/1
Content-Type: application/json
If-Match: {{order.response.headers.ETag}}
X-CSRF-TOKEN: {{token.response.body.$.token}}

{ "notes": "Leave it at the side door." }

###
# The same ETag again is stale now. Expect 412
PATCH {{host}}/api/orders/1
Content-Type: application/json
If-Match: {{order.response.headers.ETag}}
X-CSRF-TOKEN: {{token.response.body.$.token}}

{ "notes": "Ring the bell." }

###
# With the new ETag, clear the notes again.
# Expect 200 with "notes": null
PATCH {{host}}/api/orders/1
Content-Type: application/json
If-Match: {{patched.response.headers.ETag}}
X-CSRF-TOKEN: {{token.response.body.$.token}}

{ "notes": null }

###
# Create: 201 with a Location and an ETag.
# Expect 201, and it has a Location header
# @name created
POST {{host}}/api/orders
Content-Type: application/json
X-CSRF-TOKEN: {{token.response.body.$.token}}

{ "items": [ { "productId": 2, "quantity": 1 } ] }

###
# DELETE cancels. Expect 204
DELETE {{host}}/api/orders/{{created.response.body.$.id}}
X-CSRF-TOKEN: {{token.response.body.$.token}}

###
# A second DELETE meets a cancelled order. Expect 409
DELETE {{host}}/api/orders/{{created.response.body.$.id}}
X-CSRF-TOKEN: {{token.response.body.$.token}}

###
# The order is still there. Expect 200 with
# "status": "Cancelled"
GET {{host}}/api/orders/{{created.response.body.$.id}}

###
# No such route: status-code pages fill the empty 404 with
# Problem Details for a JSON client.
# Expect 404, and it carries Content-Type:
# application/problem+json
GET {{host}}/api/nope
Accept: application/json

###
# A browser gets plain text instead.
# Expect 404, and it carries Content-Type: text/plain
GET {{host}}/api/nope
Accept: text/html

###
# The Admin removes the cancelled order for good. Expect 200
POST {{host}}/dev/sign-in/avery

###
# Expect 200
# @name averyToken
GET {{host}}/api/csrf

###
# Expect 204
DELETE {{host}}/api/admin/orders/{{created.response.body.$.id}}
X-CSRF-TOKEN: {{averyToken.response.body.$.token}}

###
# Expect 204
POST {{host}}/dev/sign-out
